<title>FRANCE: French crude imports rose in April.</title>
<headline>French crude imports rose in April.</headline>
<dateline>PARIS 1997-06-19</dateline>
<text>
<p>France's industry ministry issued the following data for deliveries to refineries, margins and capacity utilisation. Crude deliveries by origin ('000 tonnes).</p>
<p>					    Apr 1997   Mar 1997 Apr 1996</p>
<p>    Total				 7,422	6,654    7,713</p>
<p>    Middle East			 2,700	2,009    2,827</p>
<p>    including Saudi Arabia	  1,491	  774    1,536</p>
<p>		  Iraq			330	  425	  0</p>
<p>		  Iran			628	  453	789</p>
<p>		  Kuwait		     13	    7	255</p>
<p>    Africa				1,295	1,410    1,197</p>
<p>    including Algeria		   353	  398	385</p>
<p>		  Libya		     319	  162	 79</p>
<p>		  Nigeria		     0	  653	566</p>
<p>    OECD Europe			 2,736	2,546    2,326</p>
<p>    including Britain		 1,305	1,109    1,157</p>
<p>		  Norway		  1,198	1,239    1,008</p>
<p>		  France		    151	  146	160</p>
<p>    Other				   690	  688    1,363</p>
<p>    including Venezuela		   0	    0	  0</p>
<p>		  Mexico			0	    0	  0</p>
<p>		  ex-USSR		   690	  688    1,363</p>
<p>The ministry also gave the following data for French refining margins, based on North Sea Brent crude ($/barrel).</p>
<p>			    May 1997  Apr 1997  Mar 1997  May 1996</p>
<p>    Refining Margin     2.26	3.31	2.62	2.56</p>
<p>In the most recent month for which refinery capacity utilisation is available, French plants ran at 75 percent of capacity in March, when several units had spring maintenance.</p>
<p>The figure was down from 89 percent in February and 96 percent in January.</p>
<p>-- Paris newsroom +331 4221 5146</p>
</text>
